---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const maslow = [
  { level: 'Self-actualisation', eg: 'Challenging projects, training', w: 40 },
  { level: 'Esteem', eg: 'Promotion, titles, praise', w: 55 },
  { level: 'Love and belonging', eg: 'Team events, friendly culture', w: 70 },
  { level: 'Safety', eg: 'Permanent contract, safe workplace', w: 85 },
  { level: 'Physiological', eg: 'A wage for food and rent', w: 100 },
];
---
<Layout title="2.4 Motivation Theories">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.4 Motivation Theories</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.4</span>
    <h1>Motivation Theories</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Taylor, Maslow and Herzberg: why people work hard, and what managers can do about it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define intrinsic and extrinsic motivation</li>
        <li>Outline Taylor, Maslow and Herzberg</li>
        <li>Apply each theory to a business, and evaluate it</li>
        <li>Recommend a theory for a manager, and justify it</li>
        <li>Explain financial and non-financial rewards, and types of training</li>
        <li>HL: McClelland, Deci and Ryan, equity and expectancy theory; labour turnover, appraisal, recruitment <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Intrinsic vs extrinsic</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Intrinsic</div>
          <p>From <strong>inside</strong> a person. Internal rewards: fun, pleasure, meaning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Extrinsic</div>
          <p>From <strong>outside</strong> a person. External rewards: money, points, praise, threats.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Taylor: scientific management (1911)</h2>
      <p>American engineer in steel mills, where workers set their own pace. He saw two problems: workers did less than they could, and even at full effort output was low. His answer: <strong>study work scientifically, and pay for results</strong>. He timed every task with a stopwatch to cut waste and find the fastest way.</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text-muted);">Context: mass factory production had arisen within a generation or two. Mechanise production... mechanise people!</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Efficiency</th><th>Motivation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>One specialised task each. Managers time and analyse it. Workers trained in that task only. No innovating.</td>
            <td>Base rate of pay, paid if a minimum target is met, plus a bonus per unit above target. Money is the motivator.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Economic man: carrot and stick</div>
        <p>Pay is the main motivator. <strong>Piece rate</strong>: pay per unit. Managers enforce targets. Still used in factories and in commission-based jobs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Workers become expert at one task. Standard products, less waste. Higher pay possible.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Boring, so demotivating. No worker ideas. Modern jobs need creativity. Ignores other motivation factors.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Maslow: hierarchy of needs (1943)</h2>
      <p>Psychologist Abraham Maslow said needs come in a set order: meet a lower need first. (The pyramid was added later by others.)</p>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; gap:4px; margin:1rem 0;">
        {maslow.map((m, i) => (
          <div style={`width:${m.w}%; background:var(--blue-pale); border:1px solid var(--blue); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; text-align:center; font-size:0.85rem;`}>
            <strong>{5 - i}. {m.level}</strong><br /><span style="color:var(--text-muted);">{m.eg}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">The needs themselves: physiological (air, food, water, sleep); safety (security, safe work, savings); love and belonging (friends, team); esteem (achievement, respect, status); self-actualisation (reaching your full potential).</p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Simple to apply (?). Shows pay is not the only need.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>The order is arguably not fixed. Differs across cultures. Hard to measure. Meeting the same need in different people takes different actions by the company.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Herzberg: two-factor theory (1959)</h2>
      <p>Frederick Herzberg interviewed about 200 accountants and engineers. He found satisfaction and dissatisfaction have <strong>different causes</strong>: motivators drive satisfaction, hygiene factors drive dissatisfaction. <strong>Fix hygiene factors first, then add motivators.</strong></p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Hygiene factors</th><th>Motivators</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Company policy, supervision, relationships, working conditions, salary, job security</td>
            <td>Achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement, growth</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Prevent dissatisfaction. Fixing them leaves staff neutral, not motivated</td>
            <td>Create satisfaction</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Simple and practical. Supports job enrichment.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Small, narrow sample. Self-reported. Salary can motivate some people.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Taylor vs Herzberg</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Taylor</th><th>Herzberg</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Pay</strong></td><td>Motivates</td><td>Only prevents dissatisfaction</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>The job</strong></td><td>Narrow, repeated tasks</td><td>Responsibility and growth</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Suits</strong></td><td>Repetitive work</td><td>Skilled work</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Where this is assessed (SL)</div>
        <p>Paper 1: case study questions, including a 10-mark extended response. Paper 2 Section B: structured questions then a 10-mark extended response, possibly as part of a more numbers-based question. IA: possible if your research question is about staff motivation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Financial and non-financial rewards</h2>
      <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div><p>This section and the next were added from the syllabus to fill gaps in the lesson slides.</p></div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Financial reward</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Salary</strong></td><td>Fixed annual pay, paid monthly</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Wages</strong></td><td>Time rate: pay per hour worked. Piece rate: pay per unit made (Taylor)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Commission</strong></td><td>A percentage of the value of sales made</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Performance-related pay (PRP)</strong></td><td>Extra pay for meeting individual targets, often set at appraisal</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Profit-related pay</strong></td><td>A share of the business's profit paid to staff</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Employee share ownership</strong></td><td>Staff are given or sold shares, so they gain when the business does</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Fringe payments</strong></td><td>Perks on top of pay: company car, health insurance, discounts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Non-financial reward</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Job enrichment</strong></td><td>More challenging, responsible work (Herzberg's motivators)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Job rotation</strong></td><td>Moving between tasks to reduce boredom</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Job enlargement</strong></td><td>More tasks at the same level</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Empowerment</strong></td><td>Freedom to make decisions about your own work</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Purpose</strong></td><td>The chance to make a difference</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Teamwork</strong></td><td>Working in groups: belonging (Maslow)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p><strong>Training:</strong> <strong>induction</strong> (introducing new staff to the business), <strong>on the job</strong> (learning while working, e.g. shadowing), <strong>off the job</strong> (courses away from the workplace).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">More theories and HR tools <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Key idea</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>McClelland: acquired needs</strong></td><td>Each person is driven mostly by one of three learned needs: achievement, affiliation or power</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Deci and Ryan: self-determination</strong></td><td>Intrinsic motivation grows when people have autonomy, competence and relatedness</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Equity theory</strong></td><td>Staff compare what they put in and get out with others; feeling unfairly treated demotivates</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Expectancy theory</strong></td><td>Effort depends on believing effort leads to performance, performance leads to a reward, and the reward is valued</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="formula-box">Labour turnover = (staff leaving in a year ÷ average number of staff) × 100</div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">High turnover raises recruitment and training costs and can signal demotivation.</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Appraisal:</strong> formative (ongoing feedback to improve), summative (a judgement at the end of a period), 360-degree (feedback from managers, peers and subordinates), self-appraisal (staff assess themselves).</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Recruitment:</strong> internal (promote or move existing staff: cheaper, known people, but leaves a gap and no new ideas) or external (adverts, agencies, job sites: new ideas and a wider pool, but slower, costlier and riskier).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Intrinsic motivation comes from inside; extrinsic from outside rewards</li>
        <li>Taylor: break work into timed tasks, pay for results; money motivates</li>
        <li>Maslow: five needs in order, lower ones first; pay isn't the only need</li>
        <li>Herzberg: hygiene factors stop dissatisfaction, motivators create satisfaction</li>
        <li>Rewards can be financial (pay, commission, shares, perks) or non-financial (enrichment, empowerment, teamwork)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Why do you work hard?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Finish each sentence, out loud with a partner or on paper.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>The class I worked hardest in last year was... because...</li>
          <li>A job I would like now is... because...</li>
          <li>If I was the boss, I would get my staff to work hard by...</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Intrinsic or extrinsic?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">A pay rise<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Enjoying a challenge<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Intrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">A bonus for hitting a target<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Feeling proud of your work<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Intrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Praise from a manager<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Avoiding a warning letter<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Taylor concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define scientific management and outline how time and motion study was used. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Breaking tasks into timed steps to find the most efficient method, then training workers in it.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how Taylor expected pay to motivate workers. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Base rate, minimum target, bonus per unit above it: more effort, more pay.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why Taylor gave managers and workers different roles. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Managers plan and analyse; workers only carry out the task.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two advantages of his approach for a business. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Higher output per worker, standard products, less waste.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two disadvantages, one for workers and one for the business. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Workers: boring, narrow skills. Business: no worker ideas, costly to set up.</span></button></li>
          <li>Is Taylor's theory intrinsic or extrinsic? Justify. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic: the reward is pay, not the work itself.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Where does Taylor fit?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each context: how does Taylor work here, how does he not, and how well does he fit (1 to 5)? Use task breakdown, time and motion study, close supervision, piece rates.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>A Victorian factory</li>
          <li>A fast food outlet</li>
          <li>A services office, e.g. an accounting firm: staff advise clients and solve a different problem each day</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answers</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Victorian factory: repetitive tasks, easy to time and pay per unit. Strong fit (4 to 5).<br />
            Fast food: standard tasks and timed steps work well; but service and staff turnover suffer from boring work. Good fit (3 to 4).<br />
            Services office: every problem is different, output is hard to count, creativity matters. Poor fit (1 to 2).
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These possible answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: To what extent?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">"Vietnam's culture is ideal for scientific management." (A major IB textbook.) To what extent do you agree?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Maslow concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>List the five levels in order, with one workplace example of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Physiological (wage for food), safety (contract), belonging (team events), esteem (promotion), self-actualisation (challenging projects).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why Maslow thought lower needs must be met first. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Unmet lower needs dominate attention, so higher rewards do not motivate yet.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two ways a business could meet esteem needs. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Public recognition and praise; promotion or a job title.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how one reward (a pay rise) could meet more than one level. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A pay rise can buy security (safety) and signal status (esteem).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two limitations of Maslow's theory. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">The order is not fixed; needs differ across people and cultures; hard to measure.</span></button></li>
          <li>What does Maslow add that Taylor misses? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Non-pay needs such as belonging and growth. Taylor mainly sees money.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 7: What would Maslow say?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Which level(s) are affected, and what would Maslow predict or advise?</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Redundancies: how will the remaining staff react?</li>
          <li>A talented employee refuses a promotion to a desk job</li>
          <li>Tight budget: cancel the staff party?</li>
          <li>An education scheme for the lowest-paid staff?</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answers</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Redundancies: safety. Remaining staff feel insecure, so higher needs stop motivating. Reassure and communicate.<br />
            Refused promotion: the job may already meet self-actualisation, and a title (esteem) isn't what they want. Shows the order isn't fixed for everyone.<br />
            Staff party: belonging. Cutting it may hurt team spirit; find a cheaper way to keep it.<br />
            Education scheme: self-actualisation, but if pay barely covers physiological and safety needs, Maslow predicts it won't motivate much yet.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These possible answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 8: Hygiene or motivator?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">Salary<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Achievement<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Company policy<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Recognition<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Working conditions<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Responsibility<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Supervision<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Growth<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 9: Herzberg concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define hygiene factor and motivator, with two examples of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene prevents dissatisfaction (salary, conditions). Motivator creates satisfaction (achievement, recognition).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why fixing hygiene factors does not motivate staff. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Removing dissatisfaction leaves staff neutral, not motivated.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain what a manager should do first and second under Herzberg, and why. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">First fix hygiene (pay, conditions), then add motivators (responsibility, growth).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why salary is a hygiene factor, and how this differs from Taylor. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Salary only prevents complaints, but Taylor treats pay as the main motivator.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two limitations of Herzberg's research. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Small sample of accountants and engineers; self-reports can be biased.</span></button></li>
          <li>Which theory best suits a software company? Justify. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Herzberg or Maslow, as staff want growth and autonomy; Taylor fits repetitive work.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 10: Southern Parcel Depot</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Southern Parcel Depot sorts parcels in Ho Chi Minh City. Staff earn a fair base wage plus a bonus for every parcel above target, and work in clean rooms on permanent contracts. Output is high, but a third of staff leave within a year, and exit surveys say work feels boring and machine-like. Three managers disagree. Ha wants bigger bonuses. Mai wants more team events and security. Vy wants staff to take responsibility and suggest improvements. The owner can afford only one change.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">For Ha, Mai and Vy: which theory is behind the idea, what's the evidence for and against, and how well does it fit (1 to 5)? Finish: The owner should back ___ because ___. However, ___.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Points to consider</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Ha (Taylor): bonuses already drive high output, but pay is already fair and staff still leave, so more money may not fix boredom.<br />
            Mai (Maslow: belonging, safety): contracts are already permanent, so safety looks met; team events may help belonging, but the surveys complain about the work, not loneliness.<br />
            Vy (Herzberg motivators; Maslow esteem and self-actualisation): hygiene factors (wage, clean rooms, contracts) look fixed already, and the complaint is boring, machine-like work, so responsibility targets the cause. However, it takes time and may not suit every task.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These points are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 11: Exam practice</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Explain one way a manager could use Herzberg's theory at Southern Parcel Depot. [4 marks]</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

